---
title: Error handling
description: Normalize WebGPU, shader, resource, and runtime failures into stable reports.
---

All adapters route initialization and render failures through `toSpektralErrorReport(error, phase)`. The resulting payload is shared by callbacks, history, the default overlay, and custom error UI.

Import `SpektralErrorReport` and the related error types from the same Svelte, React, or Vue
entrypoint as your adapter. The framework-neutral root and `/core` entrypoints export the same type
family.

## Report

```ts
interface SpektralErrorReport {
	readonly code: SpektralErrorCode;
	readonly severity: 'error' | 'fatal';
	readonly recoverable: boolean;
	readonly title: string;
	readonly message: string;
	readonly hint: string;
	readonly details: readonly string[];
	readonly stack: readonly string[];
	readonly rawMessage: string;
	readonly phase: 'initialization' | 'render';
	readonly source: SpektralErrorSource | null;
	readonly context: SpektralErrorContext | null;
	readonly shader: SpektralShaderErrorMetadata | null;
}
```

`code` is the stable routing key. Text fields are display-oriented and may contain source-mapped compiler information. `context`, when present, can include a material signature, pass-graph summary, and active render targets.

For shader failures, `shader` can identify the pass kind and label, shader stage, input and output
formats, `user` or generated `wrapper` source, and the mapped line and column. Generated wrapper
WGSL is not exposed. User-source snippets contain at most seven lines. The report and its nested
metadata, source, context, and list values are immutable snapshots.

## Codes

| Area | Codes |
| --- | --- |
| WebGPU setup | `WEBGPU_UNAVAILABLE`, `WEBGPU_ADAPTER_UNAVAILABLE`, `WEBGPU_CONTEXT_UNAVAILABLE` |
| Shader compilation | `WGSL_COMPILATION_FAILED`, `COMPUTE_COMPILATION_FAILED`, `COMPUTE_CONTRACT_INVALID`, `MATERIAL_PREPROCESS_FAILED` |
| Device and commands | `WEBGPU_DEVICE_LOST`, `WEBGPU_UNCAPTURED_ERROR`, `BIND_GROUP_MISMATCH` |
| Runtime resources | `RUNTIME_RESOURCE_MISSING`, `RESOURCE_REGISTRY_DUPLICATE`, `RESOURCE_REGISTRY_TEXTURE_MISSING`, `RESOURCE_REGISTRY_STORAGE_BUFFER_MISSING`, `UNIFORM_VALUE_INVALID`, `STORAGE_BUFFER_OUT_OF_BOUNDS`, `STORAGE_BUFFER_READ_FAILED` |
| Compute descriptors | `COMPUTE_RESOURCE_DESCRIPTOR_INVALID`, `COMPUTE_RESOURCE_UNKNOWN`, `COMPUTE_RESOURCE_INCOMPATIBLE`, `COMPUTE_RESOURCE_ALIAS_COLLISION` |
| Compute hazards and graph | `COMPUTE_RESOURCE_HAZARD`, `COMPUTE_GRAPH_MULTIPLE_WRITERS`, `COMPUTE_GRAPH_CYCLE`, `COMPUTE_RESOURCE_LIMIT_EXCEEDED` |
| External compute resources | `COMPUTE_EXTERNAL_RESOURCE_INVALID` |
| Render graph and fragment ping-pong | `RENDER_GRAPH_INVALID`, `PINGPONG_CONFIGURATION_INVALID` |
| Textures | `TEXTURE_USAGE_INVALID`, `TEXTURE_REQUEST_FAILED`, `TEXTURE_DECODE_UNAVAILABLE`, `TEXTURE_REQUEST_ABORTED` |
| Fallback | `SPEKTRAL_RUNTIME_ERROR` |

WebGPU, adapter, device-loss, and missing bitmap-decode classifications are fatal and non-recoverable where defined by the normalizer. Other known classifications and the fallback are currently reported as recoverable errors.

## Compute resource diagnostics

| Code | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `COMPUTE_RESOURCE_DESCRIPTOR_INVALID` | Invalid alias, descriptor shape, access, view range, or ping-pong pair |
| `COMPUTE_RESOURCE_UNKNOWN` | A material key does not exist |
| `COMPUTE_RESOURCE_INCOMPATIBLE` | Material access, format, usage, sample class, or sampler type cannot satisfy the descriptor |
| `COMPUTE_RESOURCE_ALIAS_COLLISION` | An alias conflicts with a reserved generated WGSL name |
| `COMPUTE_RESOURCE_HAZARD` | One dispatch samples and writes overlapping texture subresources, or binds an overlapping writable resource twice |
| `COMPUTE_GRAPH_MULTIPLE_WRITERS` | More than one pass writes the same logical resource in one graph segment |
| `COMPUTE_GRAPH_CYCLE` | Current/initial dependencies form a cycle |
| `COMPUTE_RESOURCE_LIMIT_EXCEEDED` | The resolved layout exceeds an active device limit |
| `COMPUTE_EXTERNAL_RESOURCE_INVALID` | A borrowed object, provider result, `resourceId`, device, or declared metadata is invalid |

These reports include the pass index or label, WGSL alias, and material key or external resource identity when available. Treat them as contract failures: fix the descriptor or graph instead of retrying the same frame.

Common resolutions:

- Split sampled input and storage output across two textures, or use ping-pong.
- Give consecutive processing stages separate output material keys.
- Use `version: 'initial'` only when the reader must precede the writer and no later access needs the old physical contents.
- Match filtering samplers with filterable float textures; use non-filtering samplers for integer or unfilterable-float formats.
- Recreate borrowed resources for the current `GPUDevice` after device recovery while keeping their logical `resourceId` stable.

## FragCanvas handling

| Prop | Default | Behavior |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `showErrorOverlay` | `true` | Enables default or custom error UI |
| `errorRenderer` | — | Replaces the default overlay for the adapter |
| `onError` | — | Receives the latest normalized report regardless of overlay state |
| `errorHistoryLimit` | `0` | Enables bounded history when positive |
| `onErrorHistory` | — | Receives fresh history snapshots |

History limits are floored; non-finite and non-positive values disable history. Consecutive reports with the same phase, title, message, and raw message are deduplicated. Reducing the limit trims existing history. Consumer mutation cannot change the internal snapshot. The runtime owns this sequence and calls `onErrorHistory` once for each actual history change.

The default overlay shows classification metadata, hints, technical details, source snippets, and runtime context when available. Adapter portals keep it outside canvas drawing while preserving the canvas subtree context.

When several canvases fail at once, their default overlays share one document-level stack. Only the top overlay owns focus and responds to `Escape`. Closing it focuses the previous overlay; closing the final overlay restores the original focus target and the page's prior `inert` state.

## Shader diagnostics

Compilation diagnostics retain both generated-line positions and original source locations:

- material fragment line;
- include name and line;
- define name and line;
- compute source line;
- `ShaderPass` user source line, or a generated-wrapper classification when no user line applies.

The primary diagnostic becomes `message`, remaining diagnostics become `details`, and the first mapped location builds the highlighted `source` snippet. `shader.stage` distinguishes fragment and compute failures. `ShaderPass` reports also carry the optional pass label and active input/output formats.

Spektral awaits the first preparation of every active built-in fullscreen pipeline before rendering.
After `ShaderPass.setFragment(...)`, preparation happens in the background: successful compilation
replaces the pipeline and requests a frame, while failure reports a recoverable render error and
keeps the last-known-good output. A first-preparation failure has no fallback output and blocks
initial rendering, entering the standard initialization retry/backoff path. Edits are
latest-request-wins, so stale success or failure cannot replace or report over a newer request.

Compute pipeline validation is partly asynchronous. The renderer caches pending, ready, or failed pipeline state and schedules another frame when validation completes, allowing the normalized compute compilation error to replace derivative uncaptured-command noise.

## Device loss and uncaptured errors

The renderer watches `device.lost` and `uncapturederror`, wakes manual and on-demand runtimes, and re-enters the normal reporting path. It retains up to 12 recent unique uncaptured messages, prefers a root validation cause over derivative `Invalid CommandBuffer` messages, and attaches other unique messages as details.

Device loss causes renderer recreation. Repeated creation failures use exponential delays from 250 ms to an 8-second cap; changing the pipeline signature resets the retry state.

## Production use

Route telemetry by `code`, `severity`, and `phase`. Keep the detailed overlay in development; use `errorRenderer` for product UI. Enable bounded history only when session diagnostics need it.

See [FragCanvas Reference](/docs/fragcanvas-reference#error-handling) for adapter-specific renderer types.
